---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import BrowseSidebar from "../../components/BrowseSidebar.astro";
import { GITHUB_GAME_DATA } from "../../data/github-game-data.js";
const source = `https://github.com/openfrontio/OpenFrontIO/blob/${GITHUB_GAME_DATA.revision}/src/core/configuration/Config.ts`;
---
<Layout title="Defense & Recovery — OpenFront Wiki" description="A practical OpenFront guide to Defense Posts, troop reserves, regeneration, and recovering after a failed push.">
  <SiteHeader />
  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-7xl gap-8 px-4 py-8 sm:px-6 lg:grid-cols-[230px_minmax(0,1fr)]"><aside class="lg:sticky lg:top-20 lg:h-[calc(100vh-6rem)] lg:overflow-y-auto" data-pagefind-ignore><details open><summary class="mb-3 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-cyan-glow lg:cursor-default">Browse Pages <span class="lg:hidden">▾</span></summary><BrowseSidebar /></details></aside><main class="min-w-0"><nav class="mb-6 flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-dawn/50" data-pagefind-ignore><a href="/" class="hover:text-aquarius">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/category/advanced-mechanics" class="hover:text-aquarius">Advanced Mechanics</a><span>/</span><span class="text-dawn">Defense & recovery</span></nav><header class="border-b border-white/10 pb-8"><p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-cyan-glow">Player guide</p><h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-wide text-white sm:text-4xl">Defense & recovery</h1><p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-dawn/80">Hold a front by protecting regeneration headroom, layering Defense Posts, and refusing to turn every contact into an all-in attack.</p></header><article class="wiki-content mt-8" data-pagefind-body><div class="guide-callout"><strong>The defensive habit</strong><p>Keep enough troops behind the front to answer a second attack. A border that survives one exchange but leaves you at 90% capacity and no reserve is not stable defense.</p></div><h2>Use the 42% recovery point as a reset target</h2><p>The current human regeneration curve peaks around 42% of troop capacity. After a failed push, withdraw, secure the border, and let the army return toward the 35–50% band before committing again. Do not wait for an exact percentage while an enemy is actively taking territory.</p><h2>Build depth, not a single exposed line</h2><p>The current configuration gives a Defense Post a 30-tile range. Place posts behind the expected contact line so a quick border flip does not complete construction on the wrong side. The post is a force multiplier for a contested front, not a replacement for troops.</p><h2>Recovery checklist</h2><ol><li>Stop spending troops into a stalled attack.</li><li>Keep a reserve on the threatened side of your territory.</li><li>Replace lost territory or structures before expanding again.</li><li>Watch current troops as a percentage of capacity, not as a raw number.</li><li>Resume the attack when the front converts faster than your reserve is being consumed.</li></ol><h2>Related reading</h2><p><a href="/guides/attacking-effectively">Attacking effectively</a>, <a href="/Defense_Post">Defense Post</a>, <a href="/Combat">Combat</a>, and <a href="/reference/player-state">Player State & Resources</a>.</p><p class="source-note">Current range and regeneration guidance checked against <a href={source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenFrontIO Config.ts ↗</a>.</p></article></main></div><SiteFooter />
</Layout>